{"type": "message", "id": "c892e528", "parentId": "1b08ec4d", "timestamp": "2026-04-09T15:41:55.464Z", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "7168d1279216477591860352bc4ad752", "toolName": "web_search", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"query\": \"\\\"Frequently Challenged Classics\\\" ALA list\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 4,\n  \"tookMs\": 875,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5521803443d21870\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nClassics - Banned Books Week - University Libraries at Coastal Carolina University\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5521803443d21870\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://libraries.coastal.edu/c.php?g=1263138&p=9269549\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2fd76ae28676c96c\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nA resident had objected to the novel’s depiction of how blacks are treated by members of a racist white community in an Alabama town during the Depression. The resident feared the book would upset black children reading it. Lord of the Flies by William Golding; E. M. Forster (Introduction by); E. L. Epstein (Notes by); Ben Gibson (Illustrator) -- Challenged in the Waterloo, IA schools (1992) because of profanity, lurid passages about sex, and statements defamatory to minorities, God, women and the disabled.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2fd76ae28676c96c\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"libraries.coastal.edu\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9343e2f9fafe50af\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nBook Lists - Banned Books Week - Ivy Tech Libraries at Ivy Tech Community College\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9343e2f9fafe50af\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://library.ivytech.edu/Bannedbooksweek/booklists\",